    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=x^{3}$ at the point $(-2,-8)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-2)&=3 (-2)^{2}=12, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(12)}=- \\frac{1}{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-2,-8)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(-8)&=- \\frac{1}{12}(x-(-2)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{12} - \\frac{49}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=- \\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 0+\\left(- \\frac{49}{6}\\right), \\\\y&=- \\frac{49}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,- \\frac{49}{6})$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
