  {
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - x + 1$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x - 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=4 (1) - 1=3, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(3)}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,2)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(2)&=- \\frac{1}{3}(x-(1)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{7}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{1}{3}x+\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right), \\\\x&=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(7,0)$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
