  {
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{3} - 3$ at the point $(1,-4)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=- 3 (1)^{2}=-3, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-3)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,-4)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(-4)&=\\frac{1}{3}(x-(1)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{13}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=\\frac{1}{3}x+\\left(- \\frac{13}{3}\\right), \\\\x&=13.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(13,0)$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
