    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{3} + 2$ at the point $(-2,10)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-2)&=- 3 (-2)^{2}=-12, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-12)}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-2,10)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(10)&=\\frac{1}{12}(x-(-2)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{12} + \\frac{61}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{61}{6}\\right), \\\\y&=\\frac{61}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,\\frac{61}{6})$.",
