  {
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + 2 x$ at the point $(1,4)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=4 (1) + 2=6, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(6)}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,4)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(4)&=- \\frac{1}{6}(x-(1)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{25}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{1}{6}x+\\left(\\frac{25}{6}\\right), \\\\x&=25.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(25,0)$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
