  {
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=x^{2} - x + 3$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 x - 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=2 (1) - 1=1, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(1)}=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,3)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(3)&=-1(x-(1)), \\\\y&=- x + 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=-1x+(4), \\\\x&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(4,0)$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
