    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x - 1$ at the point $(-2,-5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x - 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-2)&=- 4 (-2) - 2=6, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(6)}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-2,-5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(-5)&=- \\frac{1}{6}(x-(-2)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{6} - \\frac{16}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=- \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0+\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right), \\\\y&=- \\frac{16}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,- \\frac{16}{3})$.",
