    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=x^{3} - 3$ at the point $(2,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(2)&=3 (2)^{2}=12, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(12)}=- \\frac{1}{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(2,5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(5)&=- \\frac{1}{12}(x-(2)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{12} + \\frac{31}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=- \\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{31}{6}\\right), \\\\y&=\\frac{31}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,\\frac{31}{6})$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
