    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + 2 x + 2$ at the point $(-1,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-1)&=4 (-1) + 2=-2, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-1,2)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(2)&=\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-1)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right), \\\\y&=\\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
