    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{3}$ at the point $(-1,1)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-1)&=- 3 (-1)^{2}=-3, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-3)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-1,1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(1)&=\\frac{1}{3}(x-(-1)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right), \\\\y&=\\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,\\frac{4}{3})$.",
