    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} + x$ at the point $(-2,-10)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x + 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-2)&=- 4 (-2) + 1=9, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(9)}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-2,-10)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(-10)&=- \\frac{1}{9}(x-(-2)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{9} - \\frac{92}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{1}{9}x+\\left(- \\frac{92}{9}\\right), \\\\x&=-92.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(-92,0)$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
