    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=x^{3} + 2$ at the point $(-1,1)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-1)&=3 (-1)^{2}=3, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(3)}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-1,1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(1)&=- \\frac{1}{3}(x-(-1)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{1}{3}x+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right), \\\\x&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(2,0)$.",
