    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - x - 1$ at the point $(1,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x - 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=4 (1) - 1=3, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(3)}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,0)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(0)&=- \\frac{1}{3}(x-(1)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right), \\\\y&=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$.",
