  {
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{3}$ at the point $(2,-8)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(2)&=- 3 (2)^{2}=-12, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-12)}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(2,-8)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(-8)&=\\frac{1}{12}(x-(2)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{12} - \\frac{49}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=\\frac{1}{12}x+\\left(- \\frac{49}{6}\\right), \\\\x&=98.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(98,0)$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
