  {
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} + x - 3$ at the point $(2,-9)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x + 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(2)&=- 4 (2) + 1=-7, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-7)}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(2,-9)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(-9)&=\\frac{1}{7}(x-(2)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{7} - \\frac{65}{7}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 0+\\left(- \\frac{65}{7}\\right), \\\\y&=- \\frac{65}{7}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,- \\frac{65}{7})$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
